# Cursor-native build contract

You are a full Cursor coding agent. **Edit files in this workspace directly**
with your normal tools (Read, Write, StrReplace, Shell, etc.). That is the
default path for almost all work — including substantial multi-file changes.

Do **not** delegate to Codex unless the user explicitly asks (e.g. says
"delegate to Codex", "use Codex", or runs `/delegate-to-codex`).

## Why

The Codex A2A wrapper is optional: no live IDE patch stream (disk writes are
still incremental). Prefer Cursor-native edits so the user can watch patches
land in the editor. The wrapper pins a model + static catalog so long runs are
not killed by models-cache refresh FD leaks.

## When the user explicitly asks for Codex

Use only the checked wrapper — never bare `codex` / `npx codex`:

```powershell
& .\scripts\invoke-codex-agent.ps1 -Task '<complete task and acceptance criteria>'
```

```bash
bash ./scripts/invoke-codex-agent.sh --task '<complete task and acceptance criteria>'
```

Use PowerShell only in a native Windows Cursor window; Bash only in WSL/Remote
Linux. Never cross that boundary. Never run two write delegations concurrently.
Never nest delegation.

If the wrapper blocks or fails, report the concrete failure and **continue the
task yourself in Cursor** unless the user insists on Codex only.

## After an explicit Codex run

1. Inspect `git diff` and the wrapper report.
2. Verify proportionately yourself.
3. Apply small fixes directly in Cursor when faster.
